= Ontology =

'''Ontology concerns what kinds of things a knowledge system represents, which distinctions it makes, and what relationships can hold among them.''' In philosophy, ontology examines questions of being and existence. In knowledge representation, an ontology makes a conceptual model explicit so that people and software can describe, connect, retrieve and evaluate information consistently.

BITwiki's ontology is a model of knowledge, '''not a claim that the model exhausts reality'''. An entity type, category or semantic property can be useful without corresponding to a unique natural kind in every discipline. A classification must remain open to challenge when the evidence or purpose changes.

== What BITwiki models ==

A BITwiki article can describe a phenomenon, idea, person, institution, dataset, method, technology or another subject. The page is a representation of that subject; the two should not be confused.

{| class="wikitable"
! Question !! Representation !! Purpose
|-
| What is this page principally about? || [[Property:Entity type|Entity type]] || Distinguishes kinds of knowledge objects
|-
| Which fields study or use it? || [[Property:Domain|Domain]] || Classifies disciplinary contexts independently of identity
|-
| What claims connect it to other objects? || [[BITwiki:Relationships|Relationships]] || Makes explicitly meaningful graph connections
|-
| What evidence supports its account? || [[BITwiki:Evidence|Evidence]] and [[BITwiki:Sources|Sources]] || Grounds substantive claims
|-
| What remains uncertain or disputed? || [[BITwiki:Epistemic status|Epistemic status]] and [[BITwiki:Uncertainty|Uncertainty]] || Distinguishes support from limitations
|-
| How is the content composed and reused? || [[BITwiki:Book Matter|Book Matter]] and [[BITwiki:Transclusion|transclusion]] || Supports coherent modular authorship
|-
| How do readers find it? || Categories, portals, indices and searches || Provides navigation without changing what the subject is
|}

'''Entity type, domain, category, namespace and article structure are different dimensions.''' A subject can belong to several disciplines without being assigned several competing identities, and a portal can group different types without becoming their ontological parent.

== Types and boundaries ==

The repository's controlled vocabulary currently recognizes twelve primary entity types: Concept, Method, Protocol, Implementation, Project, Dataset, Person, Organization, Event, Publication, Location and Technology. [[BITwiki:Entity types]] defines their meanings.

Types help readers interpret an article and help tools recognize patterns across the corpus. They are '''conceptual commitments made for a purpose''', not proof of universal metaphysical boundaries. Some objects plausibly satisfy several descriptions. Choose the primary subject of the page, then express its other roles through accurate prose or separately modeled relationships.

For example, a scientific article about photosynthesis describes a biological phenomenon. A computational model of photosynthesis is a distinct representation with its own assumptions and methods. The fact that a phenomenon can be modeled does not make the phenomenon itself a computer. Both subjects can be legitimate and connected without conflating them.

The boundary between Method and Protocol is similarly contextual: a method describes an approach, while a protocol prescribes a more specific procedure or set of interaction rules. A real system may instantiate a protocol and also be a technology or an implementation; the identity chosen should match what that particular page documents.

== Relationships express meaning ==

An ontology becomes useful through the relationships it can state, not merely through a list of page types.

<pre>
specific implementation → Implements → protocol
research dataset       → Supports   → finding
knowledge artifact     → Derived from → source
subsystem              → Part of    → larger system
</pre>

These examples illustrate the intended meanings; they are not assertions that the corresponding wiki edges have already been created.

An edge such as <code>Depends on</code> makes a stronger claim than <code>Related to</code>. Its direction and scope matter. Nor does a sequence of edges necessarily license a logical inference: an intermediate relation may be conditional, context-dependent or represent a different sense of the term. See [[BITwiki:Relationships]].

== Ontology and navigation ==

Category hierarchies, subject portals and outlines help people browse knowledge. They need not reproduce an ontology's logical structure.

A category called ''Biology'' can include papers, organisms, methods, institutions, experiments and theoretical concepts. ''Biology'' is a domain or navigation grouping in this example, not an entity type shared by all those objects.

Faceted discovery can combine independent dimensions such as domain, type, review standing and relationships. The value comes from preserving the distinctions rather than compressing them into a single hierarchy. See [[BITwiki:Navigation]].

== Models, processes and uncertainty ==

Some subjects have contested boundaries: an evolving organization, a biological species concept, a system boundary, or a theory's proposed entity. BITwiki can document the different definitions, their evidence and their uses without fabricating one universally correct classification.

Process-oriented and relational approaches are important philosophical perspectives: what appears to be a stable object may also be understood through its transformations and interactions. This is a lens for inquiry, not a mandatory metaphysics for all articles.

When a distinction is disputed, explain '''what''' is disputed and which model is being applied. A semantic label does not settle that question. See [[BITwiki:Uncertainty]].

== From conceptual model to implemented schema ==

BITwiki uses Semantic MediaWiki to express page attributes and page-to-page relations in wikitext. In the source repository, <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> defines the controlled entity-type, domain and epistemic-status vocabularies and the eight named relationship metadata entries. A Lua projection in <code>Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema</code> supplies the controlled values to the knowledge-object validator.

The current <code>Template:Knowledge object</code> can emit four explicit attributes—entity type, domain, epistemic status and provenance—while <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code> checks their required values and supported controlled terms. This is '''bounded identity validation''', not a general theorem prover, ontology reasoner or guarantee of scientific accuracy.

A source-controlled property or module demonstrates repository implementation intent. The behavior of a running wiki, stored query results, inverse graph projections and external integrations require separate runtime checks before they can be described as operational.

See [[BITwiki:Semantic properties]] for the property definitions and constraints.

== Evolution and interoperability ==

A useful ontology changes carefully. A new type or relation should address a recurring modeling need, have a precise meaning and at least two credible examples, improve a real query or representation, and identify how existing annotations would be interpreted or migrated.

External vocabularies require equally careful alignment. The [https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/ W3C SKOS Reference] distinguishes broader, narrower, related, close-match and exact-match links in knowledge organization. These are not automatically interchangeable with BITwiki's properties. The [https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/ W3C PROV-O recommendation] offers a model for provenance entities, agents and activities; citing it does not assert that BITwiki has deployed an equivalent graph.

Meaning takes priority over superficial label agreement. An external mapping should explain what is equivalent, what differs and what information would be lost. See [[BITwiki:Interoperability]].

== Principles for ontological change ==

* Classify the actual subject rather than manufacturing an identity to satisfy a template.
* Keep disciplinary membership, navigation and epistemic judgment separate from entity identity.
* Prefer relationships with specific, defensible meanings.
* Distinguish facts represented in the wiki from inferences or presentation-only transformations.
* Make conceptual disagreements explicit instead of hiding them inside a schema.
* Preserve meaningful identifiers, prior formulations and provenance when definitions evolve.
* Validate technical behavior separately from the philosophical or editorial value of the model.

